'''archaize''' (är′kạ̄-īz), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''archaized'', ppr. ''archaizing''. [< Gr. ἀρχαΐζειν: see ''archaism''.] To use or imitate what is archaic; imitate an olden style; especially, to make use of archaisms in speech.

'''archaizer''' (är′kạ̄-ī-zėr), ''n.'' One who archaizes; one who affects an archaic style.
{{smaller block|But it may be remembered that Varro was himself something of an ''archaizer''. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XIV. 332.}}}}

'''archall†''', ''n.'' An old form of ''archil''.

'''archamœba''' (är-kạ-mē′bä̤), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἀρχ-, ἀρχι-, first, primitive, + NL. ''amœba''.] A hypothetical primitive simple amœba supposed by
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Archaistic Bronze Statuette from Verona, in the British Museum, in imitation of Greek work of the sixth century {{sc|b. c.}}}}}}
Haeckel to have made its appearance in the earliest geologic period, and to have been the progenitor of all other amœbæ and also of all higher forms of life.

